Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Riemannian Geometry and Approximate Fisher Metrics

Let \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d be an open parameter manifold. For a probabilistic model p_\theta(x) differentiable in \theta, the Fisher information metric is the positive semi‑definite matrix g_F(\theta) \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d} with entries

[g_F(\theta)]_{ij} = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim p_\theta}\left[\frac{\partial \log p_\theta(x)}{\partial \theta_i} \frac{\partial \log p_\theta(x)}{\partial \theta_j}\right].

We assume throughout that g_F(\theta) \succ 0 uniformly on \Theta, making (\Theta, g_F) a Riemannian manifold. The inner product on the tangent space T_\theta \Theta is \langle u, v \rangle_{g_F} = u^\top g_F(\theta) v. The natural gradient of a differentiable function f: \Theta \to \mathbb{R} is \nabla^F f(\theta) = g_F(\theta)^{-1} \nabla f(\theta), where \nabla f(\theta) is the standard Euclidean gradient. The natural gradient is the steepest ascent direction under the Fisher metric and is invariant under smooth reparameterization.

Definition 2.1 (K‑FAC Approximation). The Kronecker‑Factored Approximate Curvature (Martens & Grosse, 2015) approximates g_F(\theta) by a block‑diagonal matrix \hat{g}_F(\theta). We assume there exist constants \delta_{\text{KFAC}} \ge 0 and \kappa < \infty such that, for all \theta \in \Theta,

\|\hat{g}_F(\theta)^{-1} - g_F(\theta)^{-1}\|_{\text{op}} \le \delta_{\text{KFAC}}, \qquad \text{cond}(g_F(\theta)) \le \kappa.

Zhang et al. (2019) proved that K‑FAC converges to global minima in overparameterized neural networks with squared‑error loss and provided convergence rate bounds. Their analysis shows that K‑FAC, combined with appropriate data pre‑processing, can achieve single‑step convergence in sufficiently wide networks, a result that informs our selection of \delta_{\text{KFAC}} as a certifiable tolerance rather than a worst‑case bound.

Lemma 2.1 (Perturbation of Natural Gradient). For the exact and approximate natural gradients \Psi(\theta) = -g_F^{-1}\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta) and \hat{\Psi}(\theta) = -\hat{g}_F^{-1}\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta),

\|\Psi(\theta) - \hat{\Psi}(\theta)\| \le \delta_{\text{KFAC}} \|\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta)\|.

Proof. Immediate from operator norm properties. ∎

2.2 The K‑Stale InfoNCE Surrogate and the Information Guarantee Lemma

A central challenge in enforcing the Maakoul constraint is that the true mutual information I(Z_\theta; R_{\text{rare}}) is intractable to compute exactly. We must therefore work with a tractable lower‑bound surrogate. Crucially, an increase in this surrogate does not logically guarantee an increase in the true mutual information. We now establish the precise relationship.

Definition 2.2 (InfoNCE Lower Bound). Let Z_\theta be the latent representation produced by the encoder. Let R_{\text{rare}} \in \{0,1\} be the binary indicator of a rare event. Given a critic network T_\psi: \mathcal{Z} \times \{0,1\} \to \mathbb{R} and K negative samples, the InfoNCE lower bound is

\hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta; \psi) = \mathbb{E}_{(z, r^+) \sim p_\theta(z, r)} \left[ \log \frac{e^{T_\psi(z, r^+)}}{\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K e^{T_\psi(z, r_j)}} \right].

Oord et al. (2018) proved that \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta; \psi) \le I(Z_\theta; R_{\text{rare}}) for any \psi, with equality when the critic is optimal. However, the inequality is only a lower bound. Song and Ermon (2020) demonstrated theoretically that variational MI estimators such as MINE can exhibit variance growing exponentially with the true MI, and empirically that existing estimators fail to satisfy basic self‑consistency properties of MI such as data processing and additivity under independence.

Lemma 2.2 (Information Guarantee Lemma). Let \Delta_k = I(Z_{\theta_k}; R_{\text{rare}}) - \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta_k; \psi_k) be the gap between the true mutual information and its InfoNCE lower bound at step k. Suppose that:

1. The critic is updated every K steps to near‑optimality such that \Delta_k \le \Delta_{\max} almost surely, for some known constant \Delta_{\max}.
2. The surrogate increases by at least \eta_k \mu_k in expectation between accepted updates (Theorem 4.2).

Then, with probability at least 1 - \delta_{\text{MI}},

I(Z_{\theta_{k+1}}; R_{\text{rare}}) \ge I(Z_{\theta_k}; R_{\text{rare}}) - (\Delta_{\max} - \Delta_{k+1})^+.

In particular, if the critic is maintained such that \Delta_{k} is non‑increasing, then I(Z_{\theta_{k+1}}; R_{\text{rare}}) \ge I(Z_{\theta_k}; R_{\text{rare}}) holds.

Proof. From the definition, I = \hat{I} + \Delta. If \hat{I} increases by \eta_k \mu_k and \Delta_{k+1} \le \Delta_k, then I_{k+1} = \hat{I}_{k+1} + \Delta_{k+1} \ge \hat{I}_k + \eta_k \mu_k + \Delta_{k+1} \ge \hat{I}_k + \Delta_k = I_k. The high‑probability relaxation follows from Hoeffding bounds on the critic estimation error. ∎

This lemma, while not providing an unconditional guarantee, makes explicit the condition under which surrogate preservation translates into true MI preservation: the gap between true MI and its surrogate must be monitored and maintained, not merely assumed away. In practice, the gap \Delta_{\max} can be estimated offline via standard MI estimation benchmarks or by comparing InfoNCE with more conservative estimators such as SMILE or the variational bound of Poole et al. (2019).

Assumption 2.1 (Encoder and Critic Regularity). The encoder Z_\theta is a recurrent neural network trained with truncated backpropagation through time (BPTT) with horizon H. On any compact set, the Jacobian and Hessian of Z_\theta with respect to \theta are bounded by constants L_z(H) and L_{z2}(H). The critic T_\psi is a two‑layer MLP with C^2 activation (e.g., Swish), bounded in magnitude by T_{\max}, with its first two z‑derivatives bounded by L_T and L_{T2}.

Lemma 2.3 (Regularity of K‑Stale InfoNCE under Truncated BPTT). Under Assumption 2.1, for fixed \psi, \theta \mapsto \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta; \psi) is locally Lipschitz and almost‑everywhere differentiable. On any compact set \mathcal{K} \subset \Theta, there exists a constant L_I(\mathcal{K}, H) such that for all \theta_1, \theta_2 \in \mathcal{K},

\|\nabla_\theta \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta_1; \psi) - \nabla_\theta \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta_2; \psi)\| \le L_I(\mathcal{K}, H) \|\theta_1 - \theta_2\|.

Proof. The InfoNCE loss for a fixed sample is a composition of smooth functions through a finite computation graph induced by truncated BPTT. Uniform bounds on the derivatives follow from the boundedness of the critic and the encoder Jacobian; the Dominated Convergence Theorem then justifies exchange of expectation and differentiation. ∎

Definition 2.3 (K‑Stale InfoNCE Surrogate). The critic \psi is updated every K steps via gradient ascent on \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}. Between updates, the surrogate I_{\text{rare}}(\theta) := \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta; \psi_k) is locally Lipschitz and a.e. differentiable.

2.3 Control Barrier Functions and Probabilistic Forward Invariance

Definition 2.4 (Control Barrier Function). For a system \dot{\theta} = \Psi(\theta) and a safe set \mathcal{C} = \{\theta \in \Theta : h(\theta) \ge 0\}, the function h \in C^1 is a CBF if there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\langle \nabla h(\theta), \Psi(\theta) \rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\theta)) \quad \forall \theta \in \mathcal{C}.

A limitation of classical CBF theory is that it provides deterministic guarantees only when h is known perfectly. In high‑dimensional neural parameter spaces, hand‑crafting such barriers is intractable. Urrea et al. (2026) established rigorous probabilistic foundations for learned barrier functions, proving a probabilistic forward invariance theorem:

\mathbb{P}(\text{violation}) \le T\delta_{\text{local}} + \exp\left(-\frac{h_{\min}^2}{2 L^2 T \sigma^2}\right),

where \delta_{\text{local}} is the local approximation error rate, L is the Lipschitz constant of the learned neural barrier, and \sigma^2 quantifies the residual variance. Their framework achieved 99.8% success rate with zero violations across 648,000 time steps in human‑robot collaborative control, demonstrating that learned barriers can be practically effective even when perfect formal guarantees are not available.

For the SRI framework, we adopt this probabilistic certification model. The safe set is defined via a neural barrier function \hat{h} trained via Lipschitz‑constrained PAC learning. The continuous‑time theorems provide forward invariance with probability at least 1 - p_{\text{fail}} for any prescribed tolerance, and the discrete‑time correction step ensures that any barrier violation triggers an immediate projection back to safety.

Assumption 2.2 (Probabilistic CBF). A neural barrier function \hat{h} with known Lipschitz constant L and variance \sigma^2 is available, satisfying the probabilistic bound above with \delta_{\text{local}} vanishing as O(N^{-1/4}) in the number of training samples N.

2.4 Mangasarian‑Fromovitz Constraint Qualification and Joint Projection

The alignment manifold is G = \{\theta \in \Theta : g(\theta) = 0\} with g \in C^2 and full‑rank Jacobian. MFCQ holds at \theta \in G \cap \partial\mathcal{C} if: (a) the rows of \nabla g(\theta)^\top are linearly independent, and (b) there exists d \in \text{Null}(\nabla g(\theta)^\top) such that \langle \nabla h(\theta), d \rangle > 0.

Under MFCQ, Izmailov and Kurennoy (2014) proved that the Euclidean projection onto the feasible set defined by parametric constraints is locally unique, continuous, and locally Lipschitz. This result provides the theoretical foundation for the stability of our joint projection under small perturbations, ensuring that the SRI vector field is well‑behaved even near points where both alignment and safety constraints are active.

Lemma 2.4 (MFCQ Implies Non‑Zero Projected Safety Gradient). Under MFCQ, \Pi_\theta(\nabla h) \neq 0, where \Pi_\theta = I - g_F^{-1}\nabla g (\nabla g^\top g_F^{-1}\nabla g)^{-1}\nabla g^\top is the Fisher‑metric orthogonal projection onto T_\theta G.

Proof. If \Pi_\theta(\nabla h) = 0, then \nabla h \in \text{Range}(\nabla g), contradicting the existence of d with \langle \nabla h, d \rangle > 0 as required by MFCQ. ∎

Lemma 2.5 (Closed‑Form Joint Projection). For the QP

\Psi^* = \arg\min_{\Psi'} \frac{1}{2} \|\Psi' - \Psi\|_{g_F}^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \nabla g^\top \Psi' = 0, \quad \langle \nabla h, \Psi' \rangle \ge -\alpha(h),

the unique solution under MFCQ is \Psi^* = \Pi_\theta(\Psi) + \nu \Pi_\theta(\nabla h), with

\nu = \max\left(0, \frac{-\alpha(h) - \langle \nabla h, \Pi_\theta(\Psi) \rangle}{\|\Pi_\theta(\nabla h)\|_{g_F}^2}\right).

Proof. Standard KKT analysis with the Fisher metric; the denominator is strictly positive by Lemma 2.4. ∎

2.5 Manifold‑Constrained Optimization and Stochastic Approximation

Nagumo's Theorem (1942) guarantees that if \Psi(\theta) \in T_\theta G for all \theta \in G, then any trajectory of \dot{\theta} = \Psi(\theta) starting in G remains in G for all time. This is the geometric foundation for the alignment invariance claimed in the SRI theorems.

The discrete‑time analysis employs Doob's Martingale Convergence Theorem and Hoeffding's inequality for martingale difference sequences. A non‑negative supermartingale converges almost surely to a finite limit; for martingale differences |D_k| \le c_k, we have

\mathbb{P}\left(\sum_{i=1}^k D_i \ge \tau\right) \le \exp\left(-\frac{2\tau^2}{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2}\right).

The Riemannian natural gradient method of Zhang et al. (2024) established almost‑sure global convergence under standard smoothness assumptions, with local linear‑to‑quadratic convergence rates when the input‑output map satisfies a Riemannian Jacobian stability condition. Their analysis, which proves that this stability condition holds with high probability for sufficiently wide two‑layer networks with batch normalization, provides the convergence backbone for our discrete‑time analysis.

2.6 Relative Margin and Scale‑Invariant Dominance

Definition 2.5 (Relative Dominance Margin). The exact composite vector field \Psi_{\text{comp}} = -\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} + \lambda \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} satisfies the Dominance Lemma with relative margin \tilde{\eta} > 0 if

\langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \Psi_{\text{comp}} \rangle_{g_F} \le -\tilde{\eta} \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2, \qquad \langle \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}, \Psi_{\text{comp}} \rangle_{g_F} \ge \tilde{\eta} \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2.

This formulation scales the margin with the gradient norm, making the subsequent robustness certificate independent of gradient magnitude—a crucial practical consideration when gradients can vary by orders of magnitude across training.

