5.4 Rare‑Signal Operationalization

For the pendulum task, rare signals are operationalized as torque spikes exceeding three standard deviations from the mean control effort, occurring with probability 0.01. Generalizing to broader AGI settings, we propose an Extreme Value Theory (EVT) based definition: rare, safety‑critical events are those whose return level exceeds a calibrated threshold. Specifically, for a monitored safety metric S_t (e.g., model toxicity, hallucination rate, or physical risk indicator), rare events are defined by

R_{\text{rare}} = \mathbf{1}\{ S_t > u_\alpha \},

where the threshold u_\alpha is estimated via peaks‑over‑threshold analysis from an offline corpus of safety‑relevant interactions. This provides a principled, distribution‑agnostic method for defining R_{\text{rare}} in complex environments without requiring a fully specified generative model of rare events.

